Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Shift-Clock Chokes on Silt
- Story & Context: When a ruptured drainage valve flooded Level 9 of the Anselm Vein, foreman Declan Cruz hammered erratic triplets against a two-meter galvanized air duct with a rusted wrench to guide stranded workers through the black water. Cole Vane joined in from sixty yards away, blowing an agonizing, split-tone wail through his dented alto saxophone directly into the pipe mouth, producing a monstrous acoustic howl that smelled of machine oil and wet graphite. Crew members up at the hoist station wept thinking the deafening din was an imminent rock-burst, until Vane's raw rasp pierced the pipe intercom with the line "The seconds rot down here before they chime." The tape, caught on an inspection dictaphone, became the blueprint for the entire scene's metallic, dragged-tempo cadence.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Low-angle, gritty 35mm album artwork of a soot-crusted industrial shift-clock half-submerged in muddy underground sump water, its cracked glass face reflecting a lone miner's headlamp beam, framed by massive oxidized ventilation pipes sweating rusty condensation in cold darkness.

Track 2: "Who Stole the Daylight Shift?"
- Story & Context: Written during a grueling forty-eight-hour overtime stretch in the Badger-State shaft, bassist Ilya Moss tracked the passing days solely by tallying cuts into his leather tool belt with a sharpened cotter pin while Cruz tapped a lethargic, syncopated dirge against an overhead ventilation flange. Half-blinded by carbon arc fumes and tasting copper in his dry throat, Moss confronted the pit boss over falsified timecards, a bitter standoff that ended with Moss smashing the company time-punch register against the shaft floor. The song captures Moss's raspy, tearful throat tearing through thick baritone guitar fuzz: "I spent three whole Decembers staring at a zinc pipe." The track became an anthem for regional shift workers who had sacrificed their prime years to underground darkness.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic, melancholic album art showing a row of rusted brass punch-card slots on a timber mine wall, covered in graphite dust and yellowed time cards stamped in faded red ink, with a lone, tarnished alto saxophone resting against a stack of discarded work boots under a flickering incandescent bulb.

Track 3: Anselm Station, November 4th
- Story & Context: Months after the Anselm copper complex was permanently padlocked under bitter November frost, teenagers began lowering road flares down the abandoned ventilation flues to listen to the eerie, wind-driven hum resonating off the subterranean ductwork. Folk myth took hold that if you leaned over the grates at dusk, you could hear the lingering phantom rhythm of Cruz's pipe drumming and Vane's alto saxophone lingering in the deep draft. When crate-digger audiophiles dropped a binaural field recorder two kilometers into the abyss, they captured a bone-chilling gust of coal dust and acoustic metal decay that seemed to articulate the phrase "You're thirty years too late to punch the clock." The resulting field recording was pressed onto vinyl with a wall of crushing fuzz bass layered underneath, closing the band's catalog with mythic finality.
